### [Liquid Glass Effect](https://freefrontend.com/css-liquid-glass/#2025-06-20-liquid-glass-l)
A frosted glass button with a displacement map glitch effect on hover. The `.glass` button has a circular shape, semi-transparent white background, backdrop-filter (referencing an SVG `#frosted` filter), and two white lines forming a crosshair. The SVG filter uses `feImage` with a PNG data URL as displacement map, `feGaussianBlur`, and `feDisplacementMap`. On `mouseover`, the displacement `scale` animates from `1` to `1.4`; on `mouseout`, back to `1`. This creates a rippling, distorted “wet glass” effect.

### [Liquid Glass with Scroll and Drag](https://freefrontend.com/css-liquid-glass/#2025-06-20-liquid-glass-scroll-drag-configure-chromium-l)
A customizable glass displacement effect using SVG filters with real-time controls. A draggable glass surface (`backdrop-filter: url(#filter)`) applies chromatic displacement via `feDisplacementMap` and a dynamically generated SVG displacement image (linear gradients for R/G/B channels).

## Frequently Asked Questions
The `<feDisplacementMap>` element distorts the pixels of the underlying backdrop image according to the color values of a displacement map source, typically driven by SVG noise. Combining this displacement with a standard `backdrop-filter: blur()` mimics true physical light refraction, warping the background content directly around the curved, liquid edges of your UI containers.

Simultaneously applying real-time backdrop blurs and pixel-shifting displacement maps requires intensive GPU rasterization.

Implement a defensive progressive enhancement strategy using an `@supports (backdrop-filter: blur(1px)) and (filter: url('#liquid-glass-refract'))` query.

